\section{\VAR{sections[0] | tex}}
If $P$ and $Q$ are regular expressions and $P$ does not contain the empty string
($\varepsilon \notin P$), then the equation $R = Q + RP$ has the unique solution
\[
  R = Q + RP \;\Rightarrow\; R = QP^{*}.
\]
\paragraph{Method.}
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Write one equation per state.} A state's right-hand side sums its incoming
        transitions: each move $q_j \xrightarrow{a} q_i$ adds the term $q_j a$.
        The start state also gets $\varepsilon$.
  \item \textbf{Solve by substitution.} Substitute equations into each other; whenever a
        state refers to itself, $q = Q + qP$, Arden's theorem gives $q = QP^{*}$.
  \item \textbf{Combine.} The regular expression is the union of the solutions of all
        final states ($\varnothing$ if there are none).
\end{enumerate}

